@"A type in the type system consisting of a string *name* that's either 'literal'(string) or 'node <node-id>' "
+"or their arrays by appending '[]', can be multiple for higher dimensional array"
node type with name(literal)

@"For a natural language simple expectation of a node type, its actual type is a standardized node type"
rule node-type for nlType(literal)
given:
  represents: ref(literal) "An atomic, domain-independent object which {$for.nlType} tries to represent"
  id: ref(literal) "The hyphen-separated id describing the represented object: {$given.represents}"
must be:
  node type with name = "node {$given.id}"

@"A rule spec consisting of a *subject* kind to be standardized, "
+"a *subjectParams* description of the inputs/context needed to retrieve or derive the concrete subject instances, "
+"a *type* the standardized instance will be of, and a *description* detailing the standardization logic using those params"
node rule-spec with
  subject(literal), subjectParams(literal), type(node type), description(literal)

@"For a natural language rule, its rule spec is a standardized node rule-spec"
rule rule-spec for nlRule(literal)
must be: ref(node rule-spec) 
            "For {$for.nlRule}, the subject identifies a kind of object that needs constraint, "
           +"subjectParams provides the necessary inputs or contextual data from which concrete subject instances can be extracted or constructed. "
           +"When it's standardized, it would be of an expected type, and an inner description of what the standardization logic"

@"A tuple consisting of a pair of literal values"
node tuple<t1, t2> with val1(t1), val2(t2)

@"A blueprint value in a rule, encoded as an expression string, yielding a concrete value after binding 'for' parameters. Must be one of"
+"primitive: variable(($for|$given).<key>.fields), literal(var-interpolated string using `{}`), array, or 'ref(<type>) \"var-interpolated desc\"' for a described object of expected type. "
+"dynamic: 'node <node-id> with key=value,...' | 'satisfying <rule-id> where key=value,...' | 'result of <action-id> where key=value,...', passing primitive expr args. "
+"sequential: 'seq' + a dynamic expr, with optional 'iter' prefix on args (key=[iter] value), producing a sequence, and setting `iter` makes an array arg finally passed as each of its element"
node rule-value with expr(literal)

@"Has two generic type params tk and tv, accepts a *value* tk, a dictionary *branches* of tk-tv pairs, then returns the tv-value of the pair whose key is the given tk-key"
action switch-cases<tk, tv> on value(tk), branches(node tuple<tk, tv>[]) returns: tv

@"For a rule value spec, its actual rule value implementation is a rule value node"
rule rule-value for valueSpec(literal)
given:
  analysis: ref(node tuple<literal,literal>) 
               "Classification of {$for.valueSpec} into 'primitive'|'dynamic'|'sequential' (first element) and a short analysis chain (second element)"
  primitive:
    node tuple<literal,literal> with val1="primitive",
      val2="primitive, identify whether it is a variable reference (use $for.xxx or $given.xxx), "
          +"a literal string (wrap in single quotes), an array (list elements), or a described object (use ref(<type>) \"description\"). "
          +"Use the analysis to decide the exact primitive form."
  dynamic:
    node tuple<literal,literal> with val1="dynamic",
      val2="dynamic, determine the operation kind ('node','satisfying','result of'), the target id, "
          +"and the key=value pairs where values are primitive expressions. "
          +"Assemble as '<op> <id> with|where k=v, ...' using the analysis for any details."
  sequential:
    node tuple<literal,literal> with val1="sequential",
      val2="sequential, follow the dynamic pattern but prefix 'seq', and decide if any key should have 'iter' prefix "
          +"(meaning the value is an array to iterate over). "
          +"Form: 'seq <op> <id> with|where k=[iter]v, ...' as appropriate."
  branch: result of switch-cases where value=$given.analysis.val1, cases=[$given.primitive, $given.dynamic, $given.sequential]
must be:
  ref(node rule-value) "An expr describing {$for.valueSpec}, {$given.branch}. Analysis: {$given.analysis.val2}"

@"A rule consisting of an *annotation* describing 'a (possibly parameterized) subject must be what a standardized value', "
+"an *id*, a dictionary *for* of key-type parametrizing the subject, "
+"a dictionary *given* of key-ruleValue for the intermediate steps, "
+"and *mustBe* of a ruleValue as the final standardized value of the subject"
node rule-meta with
  annotation(literal), id(literal),
  for(node tuple<literal, node type>[]),
  given(node tuple<literal, node rule-value>[]),
  mustBe(node rule-value)

rule rule-meta for spec(node rule-spec)
given:
  annotation: ref(literal)
                 "A single-line string close to '(For {$for.spec.subjectParams}) {$for.spec.subject} must be {$for.spec.type}', "
                +"slightly modified for semantical smoothness"
  id: ref(literal) "A hyphen-separated string id for {$for.spec.subject}"
  for: ref(node tuple<literal, node type>[])
          "A dictionary of key-type for subject params: {$for.spec.subjectParams} parametrizing {$for.spec.subject}, "
         +"where a kv-pair consists of a string camelCase key for a parameter and a value of the type needed for the param"
  interalSteps: ref(node tuple<literal, literal>[]) "A dictionary"
  